Third-party material
--------------------

MAC address vendor table (IEEE MA-L / OUI public listing)
  WireForge embeds a compact table that maps the first three bytes of a MAC
  address to the name of the organisation the block is assigned to
  (crates/wf-wifi/data/oui.bin: 39,948 prefixes, 19,670 names).
  Source: IEEE Registration Authority, MA-L (OUI) public listing,
  https://standards-oui.ieee.org/oui/oui.csv, downloaded 2026-11-16.
  Changes: organisation addresses dropped; names tidied for display (legal-form
  suffixes removed, all-capitals names turned into title case, long names
  shortened); 549 rows owned by "Private" or the IEEE Registration Authority
  itself left out. Details and how to regenerate the table are in
  crates/wf-wifi/data/SOURCE.txt.
  The listing is published by the IEEE as public registry information. IEEE is
  not affiliated with WireForge and does not endorse it. Vendor names are
  shown only to help identify devices and may be out of date or wrong for a
  particular device.

Rust crates
  WireForge is built with open-source Rust crates, among them eframe / egui,
  image, rfd, serde, serde_json, qrcode, raw-window-handle, tiny-skia,
  embed-resource and the windows crate. Each is used under its own licence
  (MIT and/or Apache-2.0 for most); see Cargo.lock for the exact versions and
  each crate for its terms.

Fonts
  WireForge loads the Segoe UI and Segoe MDL2 Assets / Segoe Fluent Icons
  fonts that are installed with Windows at run time. They are not distributed
  with WireForge.

Online services (used only when you start them)
-----------------------------------------------

Measurement Lab (M-Lab) NDT7 speed test
  https://www.measurementlab.net/
  The speed test uses M-Lab's public measurement platform through its Locate
  API and the open ndt7 protocol, as M-Lab invites client developers to do
  (https://www.measurementlab.net/develop/). WireForge follows M-Lab's rules
  for clients: a test only ever starts when the user clicks, the user is
  shown M-Lab's data policy and must agree before the first test, and no
  more than 40 tests a day are run.
  M-Lab publishes all test results, including the IP address the test came
  from, as open data and keeps them indefinitely:
  Privacy policy: https://www.measurementlab.net/privacy/
  Acceptable use policy: https://www.measurementlab.net/aup/
  WireForge's own privacy statement is in PRIVACY.txt.

ipify public IP address API
  https://www.ipify.org/
  Asked once each time the user clicks "Show" next to "Public IP address".
  The service answers with the address the request came from.

Tools the user runs (ping, traceroute, DNS lookup, whois, port check,
Wake-on-LAN) contact exactly the hosts and servers the user enters or picks:
the system DNS server or a public resolver chosen in the list (Cloudflare
1.1.1.1, Google 8.8.8.8, Quad9 9.9.9.9), and for whois the IANA whois server
and the registry it refers to.

WireForge is not affiliated with or endorsed by any of these services.
